   "subject": "Re: Differences using for loops versus purrr::map",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">If you want to find out what is going on, you can fit your model to one data set at a time to see which one causes the (possible) problem.  Perhaps you have too small a sample, or the model is not empirically identified for some other reason.  You haven&#39;t provided any information about your model or data to make educated guesses, so try asking a question about a single data set and provide the syntax for that example to show what is happening.  <br><br><div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792422505524000&amp;usg=AOvVaw20wUlUNc2Non2r0TrNeYHo\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div><div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
